@@host: That is all Caesar says of the moment. He does not describe the man.

@@host: Vercingetorix was taken to Rome in chains, and kept in a prison cell for six years. In forty-six before Christ he was led through the streets in Caesar's triumph, and then put to death.

@@host: There was fighting for one more summer, but Gaul never again rose as one. Over the centuries that followed, its people came to speak Latin, and their Latin would become French.

@@host: In eighteen sixty-five, the emperor Napoleon the Third, who was writing his own life of Caesar, had a bronze statue of Vercingetorix raised on the hill above the village of Alise-Sainte-Reine, where the excavations had found the Roman ditches. He stands there still, looking out over the plain.

